>> title: Lotus Seed Paste
>> category: Custards & Puddings
>> tags: lotus, sweet, filling, mooncake, stovetop, make-ahead
>> counters: Bakery, Dim Sum Counter
>> aka: lotus paste, lian rong, lin yung, sweet lotus seed paste, mooncake filling
>> servings: 16
>> time: 6 hr 30 min
>> slack: narrow — the germs come out of every seed or the paste is bitter through, and no amount of sugar sweetens past it

>> step: soak 4 hr, pull the germs
Soak @dried split lotus seeds{2%cup}(300 g) in @water for the soak{6%cup}(1.4 L) ~soak{4%hr}, then open each seed and flick out the green germ down its middle.

>> step: simmer 60 min
Drain @&(~1)seeds{}, cover them with @water for the simmer{8%cup}(1.9 L) and ~simmer{60%min} until a seed collapses under the back of a spoon with no resistance at all.

>> step: blend smooth
Blend @&(~1)soft seeds{} with a ladle of their own cooking water until there is no grain left in it.

>> step: fry down with the sugar 30 min
Fry @&(~1)purée{} in a #wok{} with @rock sugar{1 1/4%cup}(250 g), @maltose syrup{2%Tbs}(40 g) and @fine sea salt{1/4%tsp}, working in @peanut oil{1/3%cup}(80 mL) a spoonful at a time and waiting for each to be taken up, ~fry{30%min} until the paste pulls away from the wok in one mass.

>> step: cool 1 hr
Cool @&(~1)paste{} ~cool{1%hr}.
